---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro'
import ActionBox from '../../components/ActionBox.astro'
import ServiceEntitlement from '../../components/ServiceEntitlement.astro'
import ServiceShot from '../../components/ServiceShot.astro'
import FlowDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/FlowDiagram.astro'

// Every screenshot below is captured by scripts/capture-services.ts,
// which performs the act and asserts the surface before capturing; the
// date travels with each shot. Re-run the script to refresh the evidence.
const captured = '2026-08-29'
---

<Base
  title="The status service"
  description="The live health of the OIML SMART estate: twelve probed services with content assertions, declared objectives against measured actuals, a human-written incident record, and RSS/Atom subscription."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Services"
    title="The status service"
    lede="One page answers 'is it up?': every public service in the estate probed once a minute, the declared availability and latency objectives next to the measured numbers, and a human-written incident record. No account needed, ever."
  />

  <section class="px-8 pb-20 max-w-5xl">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <ActionBox
        href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/"
        label="Open the status service"
        description="The live estate health. Public; the incident timeline is subscribable as RSS or Atom."
      />
      <ServiceShot
        service="status"
        id="status-overview"
        alt="The status page: All systems operational, with the twelve probe rows across identity, the platform instances, the AI service, and the public surfaces"
        caption="All twelve probe rows, each with its measured uptime and latency"
        date={captured}
      />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">What you can do</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-8">
        Six acts, each performed against the live page before this page listed it; the dated capture is the audit artifact, re-produced by <span class="font-mono text-sm">scripts/capture-services.ts</span> on every refresh.
      </p>
      <ol class="grid gap-10 list-none pl-0">
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">1. See the whole estate at a glance.</strong> Twelve probe rows cover the identity service (discovery and sign-in separately), the platform hub and the demo/NMI/TL instances, the AI assistant, and the public surfaces (this site, the vocabulary, the studio viewer, the CNML docs, the concepts registry). One banner answers the only question that matters at 8:55 before a presentation.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="status"
            id="status-overview"
            alt="The status page banner: All systems operational, all 12 probed services passing their content assertions"
            caption="The banner and the rows: identity, instances, public surfaces"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">2. Read what a probe actually checks.</strong> A probe is not a bare 200: each row carries a content assertion (the identity row asserts the discovery document, the sign-in row asserts the interactive island), so a page that answers with the wrong document fails honestly. Each row shows its last probe, its 30-day p95, and its 30/90-day uptime.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">3. Hold the estate to its declared objectives.</strong> The objectives table puts the declared availability and latency next to the measured actuals, per service, and a number is never rounded up to meet a target.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="status"
            id="status-objectives"
            alt="The objectives-versus-reality table: declared availability and latency per service against the measured 30-day numbers, each marked meeting"
            caption="Declared versus measured, per service"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">4. Read the incident record.</strong> Written by a human after the fact: the window, the impact, the root cause, the resolution, the follow-ups. When nothing has broken, the page says so, which is its own kind of evidence.
          </p>
          <ServiceShot
            service="status"
            id="status-incidents"
            alt="The incident record section: No incidents on record, with the note that a human writes up the window, impact, root cause, resolution, and follow-ups when something breaks"
            caption="The incident record: human-written, and honest when empty"
            date={captured}
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">5. Subscribe by feed.</strong> The incident timeline publishes as <a href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/feed.xml" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">RSS 2.0</a> and <a href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/feed.atom" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">Atom</a>; both feeds answered 200 at capture time. Email subscription rides the estate mailer posture and lands with the status tail wave; until then the page says feeds, because feeds are what exist.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-3">
            <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">6. Check the checker.</strong> The page publishes its own last-probe-run timestamp; if that timestamp goes stale, the prober itself is down and the page is showing the last known state. The freshness of the page is itself on the page.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">How it works</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-4">
        One content-asserting request per service every sixty seconds, kept for ninety days; the page is generated from those probes, and the objectives table is computed from the same measurements it is checked against.
      </p>
      <FlowDiagram
        caption="FIG. THE PROBE LOOP"
        steps={[
          { label: 'The prober', sub: 'every 60 seconds', accent: 'brand' },
          { label: 'Content assertions', sub: 'the right document, not any 200', accent: 'teal' },
          { label: 'The page and feeds', sub: 'declared versus measured', accent: 'amber' },
          { label: 'The incident record', sub: 'human-written, after the fact', accent: 'slate' },
        ]}
        arrows={[
          { label: 'probes', detail: '12 services, kept 90 days' },
          { label: 'publishes', detail: 'uptime, p95, objectives' },
          { label: 'explains', detail: 'window, impact, root cause' },
        ]}
      />
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        The status service does not probe itself: its page is the probe output, and adding a row for itself would assert what it cannot independently check. Its software (<span class="font-mono text-sm">oimlsmart/status</span>) is a member repository, source available to members.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">SMART today, SMART+ next</h2>
      <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6 mb-4">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.12em] text-ink-muted mb-2">SMART · LIVE TODAY</p>
        <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          All twelve rows above are the live SMART estate: the certification services, the identity service, and the public surfaces, each probed with its content assertion.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-dashed border-rule rounded-md p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.12em] text-ink-muted mb-2">SMART+ · THE ROADMAP TIER</p>
        <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          As the SMART+ surfaces ship (the monitored twin fleet, the SST instruments beyond the pilot), they join the probe list with the same treatment: a declared objective, a measured actual, and an honest incident record. That extension is <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">roadmap</strong>, anchored on <a href="/technologies/smart-twin" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">the SMART Twin page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Who can use it, who can run it</h2>
      <ServiceEntitlement service="status-service" />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">The honest questions</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4">
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Why is there no status row for the status service?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Because it cannot independently check itself. The page is the probe output; the last-probe-run timestamp at the bottom is the honest self-check, and a stale timestamp tells you the prober is down.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Are the numbers ever rounded to look better?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">No. Percentages truncate at two decimals, the p95 is computed from bucketed per-minute probe latencies, and a number is never rounded up to meet a target. The page says so itself, under the objectives table.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-rule rounded-md bg-paper-soft p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-[1.125rem] mb-2">Can I get email alerts instead of a feed?</h3>
          <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Not yet. Email subscription rides the estate mailer posture and lands with the status tail wave; the RSS and Atom feeds are what exist today, and the page will say email when email exists.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Where to go next</h2>
      <ActionBox
        href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/"
        label="Check the estate's pulse"
        description="The live page. No account; the feeds are one click from the subscribe section."
      />
      <ActionBox
        href="https://demo.oimlsmart.org/"
        label="Try the demo"
        description="The demo instance the status page probes. Sign in with a one-click demo account."
      />
      <ActionBox
        href="mailto:info@oimlsmart.org"
        label="Talk to us"
        description="info@oimlsmart.org, the programme's front-door address. Incident questions and feed requests land here."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-rule pt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Verified {captured}: the page answered live with all twelve probe rows operational, the declared-versus-actual objectives table, the empty incident record, and both feeds (RSS and Atom) returning 200; the captures and their assertions are in public/img/services/manifest.json.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
